# BOOK	022	SOL	Song of Solomon	Song of Solomon
022	SOL	001	002	Bride: May he kiss me with the kiss of his mouth. Groom to Bride: So much better than wine are your breasts,
022	SOL	001	003	fragranced with the finest perfumes. Bride to Groom: Your name is oil that has been poured out; therefore, the maidens have loved you.
022	SOL	001	004	Draw me forward. Chorus to Bride: We will run after you in the odor of your perfumes. Bride to Chorus: The king has led me into his storerooms. Chorus to Bride: We will exult and rejoice in you, remembering your breasts above wine. Groom to Bride: The righteous love you.
022	SOL	001	005	Bride to Chorus: O daughters of Jerusalem: I am black, but shapely, like the tabernacles of Kedar, like the tents of Solomon.
022	SOL	001	006	Do not be concerned that I am dark, for the sun has changed my color. The sons of my mother have fought against me. They have made me the keeper of the vineyards. My own vineyard I have not kept.
022	SOL	001	007	Bride to Groom: Reveal to me, you whom my soul loves, where you pasture, where you recline at midday, lest I begin to wander after the flocks of your companions.
022	SOL	001	008	Groom to Bride: If you yourself do not know, O most beautiful among women, then go out and follow after the steps of the flocks, and pasture your young goats beside the tabernacles of the shepherds.
022	SOL	001	009	O my love, I have compared you to my company of horsemen against the chariots of Pharaoh.
022	SOL	001	010	Your cheeks are beautiful, like those of a turtledove. Your neck is like a bejeweled collar.
022	SOL	001	011	Chorus to Bride: We will fashion for you chains of gold, accented with reddened silver.
022	SOL	001	012	Bride to Chorus: While the king was taking his rest, my aromatic ointment sent forth its odor.
022	SOL	001	013	My beloved is a bundle of myrrh to me. He shall abide between my breasts.
022	SOL	001	014	My beloved is a cluster of Cyprus grapes to me, in the vineyards of Engaddi.
022	SOL	001	015	Groom to Bride: Behold, you are beautiful, O my love. Behold, you are beautiful. Your eyes are those of a dove.
022	SOL	001	016	Bride to Groom: Behold, you are handsome, O my beloved, and graceful. Our bed is flourishing.
022	SOL	001	017	Groom to Bride: The timbers of our houses are of cedar; our ceilings are of cypress.
022	SOL	002	001	Bride: I am a flower of the open field and a lily of the steep valleys.
022	SOL	002	002	Groom: Like a lily among the thorns, so is my loved one among the daughters.
022	SOL	002	003	Bride to Chorus: Like an apple tree among the trees of the forest, so is my beloved among the sons. I sat beneath the shadow of the one whom I desired, and his fruit was sweet to my palate.
022	SOL	002	004	He brought me into the storeroom of wine. He set charity in order within me.
022	SOL	002	005	Prop me up with flowers. Close me in with apples. For I languish through love.
022	SOL	002	006	His left hand is under my head, and his right hand shall embrace me.
022	SOL	002	007	Groom to Chorus: I bind you by oath, O daughters of Jerusalem, by the does and the stags of the open field, not to disturb or awaken the beloved, even for as long as she wills.
022	SOL	002	008	Bride to Chorus: The voice of my beloved! Behold, he arrives leaping along the mountains, jumping across the hills.
022	SOL	002	009	My beloved is like a doe and like a young stag. Lo, he stands beyond our wall, gazing through the windows, watching through the lattices.
022	SOL	002	010	Lo, my beloved speaks to me: Groom to Bride: Rise up, quickly, my love, my dove, my shapely one, and advance.
022	SOL	002	011	For winter has now past; the rain has decreased and gone away.
022	SOL	002	012	The flowers have appeared in our land; the time for pruning has arrived. The voice of the turtledove has been heard in our land.
022	SOL	002	013	The fig tree has brought forth its green figs; the flowering vines bestow their odor. Rise up, my love, my brilliant one, and advance.
022	SOL	002	014	My dove in the clefts of the rock, in the hollows of the wall, reveal to me your face. Let your voice sound in my ears. For your voice is sweet, and your face is graceful.
022	SOL	002	015	Chorus to Groom and Bride: Capture for us the little foxes, which are tearing down the vines; for our vineyard has flourished.
022	SOL	002	016	Bride to Chorus: My beloved is for me, and I am for him. He pastures among the lilies,
022	SOL	002	017	until the day rises and the shadows decline. Bride to Groom: Return, O my beloved. Be like a doe and like a young stag upon the mountains of Bether.
022	SOL	003	001	Bride: On my bed, throughout the night, I sought him whom my soul loves. I sought him, and did not find him.
022	SOL	003	002	I will rise up, and I will circle through the city. Through the side streets and thoroughfares, I will seek him whom my soul loves. I sought him, and did not find him.
022	SOL	003	003	The watchers who guard the city found me: “Have you seen him whom my soul loves?”
022	SOL	003	004	When I had passed by them a little, I found him whom my soul loves. I held him, and would not release him, until I would bring him into my mother’s house, and into the chamber of her who bore me.
022	SOL	003	005	Groom to Chorus: I bind you by oath, O daughters of Jerusalem, by the does and the stags of the open field, not to disturb or awaken the beloved, until she wills.
022	SOL	003	006	Chorus to Groom: Who is she, who ascends through the desert, like a staff of smoke from the aromatics of myrrh, and frankincense, and every powder of the perfumer?
022	SOL	003	007	Chorus to Bride: Lo, sixty strong ones, out of all the strongest in Israel, stand watch at the bed of Solomon,
022	SOL	003	008	all holding swords and well-trained in warfare, each one’s weapon upon his thigh, because of fears in the night.
022	SOL	003	009	Bride to Chorus: King Solomon made himself a portable throne from the wood of Lebanon.
022	SOL	003	010	He made its columns of silver, the reclining place of gold, the ascent of purple; the middle he covered well, out of charity for the daughters of Jerusalem.
022	SOL	003	011	O daughters of Zion, go forth and see king Solomon with the diadem with which his mother crowned him, on the day of his espousal, on the day of the rejoicing of his heart.
022	SOL	004	001	Groom to Bride: How beautiful you are, my love, how beautiful you are! Your eyes are those of a dove, except for what is hidden within. Your hair is like flocks of goats, which ascend along the mountain of Gilead.
022	SOL	004	002	Your teeth are like flocks of shorn sheep, which ascend from the washing, each one with its identical twin, and not one among them is barren.
022	SOL	004	003	Your lips are like a scarlet ribbon, and your eloquence is sweetness. Like a piece of pomegranate, so are your cheeks, except for what is hidden within.
022	SOL	004	004	Your neck is like the tower of David, which was built with ramparts: a thousand shields are hanging from it, all the armor of the strong.
022	SOL	004	005	Your two breasts are like two young does, twins that pasture among the lilies.
022	SOL	004	006	Until the day rises and the shadows decline, I will go to the mountain of myrrh and to the hill of frankincense.
022	SOL	004	007	You are totally beautiful, my love, and there is no blemish in you.
022	SOL	004	008	Advance from Lebanon, my spouse, advance from Lebanon, advance. You shall be crowned at the head of Amana, near the summit of Senir and Hermon, by the dens of lions, by the mountains of leopards.
022	SOL	004	009	You have wounded my heart, my sister, my spouse. You have wounded my heart with one look of your eyes, and with one lock of hair on your neck.
022	SOL	004	010	How beautiful are your breasts, my sister, my spouse! Your breasts are more beautiful than wine, and the fragrance of your ointments is above all aromatic oils.
022	SOL	004	011	Your lips, my spouse, are a dripping honeycomb; honey and milk are under your tongue. And the fragrance of your garments is like the odor of frankincense.
022	SOL	004	012	An enclosed garden is my sister, my spouse: an enclosed garden, a sealed fountain.
022	SOL	004	013	You send forth a paradise of pomegranates along with the fruits of the orchard: Cypress grapes, with aromatic oil;
022	SOL	004	014	aromatic oil and saffron; sweet cane and cinnamon, with all the trees of Lebanon; myrrh and aloe, with all the best ointments.
022	SOL	004	015	The fountain of the gardens is a well of living waters, which flow forcefully from Lebanon.
022	SOL	004	016	Rise up, north wind, and advance, south wind. Send a breeze through my garden, and carry its aromatic scents.
022	SOL	005	001	Bride: May my beloved enter into his garden, and eat the fruit of his apple trees. Groom to Bride: I have arrived in my garden, O my sister, my spouse. I have harvested my myrrh, with my aromatic oils. I have eaten the honeycomb with my honey. I have drunk my wine with my milk. Eat, O friends, and drink, and be inebriated, O most beloved.
022	SOL	005	002	Bride: I sleep, yet my heart watches. The voice of my beloved knocking: Groom to Bride: Open to me, my sister, my love, my dove, my immaculate one. For my head is full of dew, and the locks of my hair are full of the drops of the night.
022	SOL	005	003	Bride: I have taken off my tunic; how shall I be clothed in it? I have washed my feet; how shall I spoil them?
022	SOL	005	004	My beloved put his hand through the window, and my inner self was moved by his touch.
022	SOL	005	005	I rose up in order to open to my beloved. My hands dripped with myrrh, and my fingers were full of the finest myrrh.
022	SOL	005	006	I opened the bolt of my door to my beloved. But he had turned aside and had gone away. My soul melted when he spoke. I sought him, and did not find him. I called, and he did not answer me.
022	SOL	005	007	The keepers who circulate through the city found me. They struck me, and wounded me. The keepers of the walls took my veil away from me.
022	SOL	005	008	I bind you by oath, O daughters of Jerusalem, if you find my beloved, announce to him that I languish through love.
022	SOL	005	009	Chorus to Bride: What kind of beloved is your beloved, O most beautiful among women? What kind of beloved is your beloved, so that you would bind us by oath?
022	SOL	005	010	Bride: My beloved is white and ruddy, elect among thousands.
022	SOL	005	011	His head is like the finest gold. His locks are like the heights of palm trees, and as black as a raven.
022	SOL	005	012	His eyes are like doves, which have been washed with milk over rivulets of waters, and which reside near plentiful streams.
022	SOL	005	013	His cheeks are like a courtyard of aromatic plants, sown by perfumers. His lips are like lilies, dripping with the best myrrh.
022	SOL	005	014	His hands are smoothed gold, full of hyacinths. His abdomen is ivory, accented with sapphires.
022	SOL	005	015	His legs are columns of marble, which have been established over bases of gold. His appearance is like that of Lebanon, elect like the cedars.
022	SOL	005	016	His throat is most sweet, and he is entirely desirable. Such is my beloved, and he is my friend, O daughters of Jerusalem.
022	SOL	006	001	Chorus to Bride: Where has your beloved gone, O most beautiful among women? To where has your beloved turned aside, so that we may seek him with you?
022	SOL	006	002	Bride: My beloved has descended to his garden, to the courtyard of aromatic plants, in order to pasture in the gardens and gather the lilies.
022	SOL	006	003	I am for my beloved, and my beloved is for me. He pastures among the lilies.
022	SOL	006	004	Groom to Bride: My love, you are beautiful: sweet and graceful, like Jerusalem; terrible, like an army in battle array.
022	SOL	006	005	Avert your eyes from me, for they have caused me to fly away. Your hair is like a flock of goats, which have appeared out of Gilead.
022	SOL	006	006	Your teeth are like a flock of sheep, which have ascended from the washing, each one with its identical twin, and not one among them is barren.
022	SOL	006	007	Like the skin of a pomegranate, so are your cheeks, except for your hiddenness.
022	SOL	006	008	There are sixty queens, and eighty concubines, and maidens without number.
022	SOL	006	009	One is my dove, my perfect one. One is her mother; elect is she who bore her. The daughters saw her, and they proclaimed her most blessed. The queens and concubines saw her, and they praised her.
022	SOL	006	010	Chorus to Groom: Who is she, who advances like the rising dawn, as beautiful as the moon, as elect as the sun, as terrible as an army in battle array?
022	SOL	006	011	Bride: I descended to the garden of nuts, in order to see the fruits of the steep valleys, and to examine whether the vineyard had flourished and the pomegranates had produced buds.
022	SOL	006	012	I did not understand. My soul was stirred up within me because of the chariots of Amminadab.
022	SOL	006	013	Chorus to Bride: Return, return, O Sulamitess. Return, return, so that we may consider you. Chorus to Groom: What will you see in the Sulamitess, other than choruses of encampments?
022	SOL	007	001	Chorus to Bride: How beautiful are your footsteps in shoes, O daughter of a ruler! The joints of your thighs are like jewels, which have been fabricated by the hand of an artist.
022	SOL	007	002	Your navel is a round bowl, never lacking in curvature. Your abdomen is like a bundle of wheat, surrounded with lilies.
022	SOL	007	003	Your two breasts are like two young twin does.
022	SOL	007	004	Your neck is like a tower of ivory. Your eyes like the fish ponds at Heshbon, which are at the entrance to the daughter of the multitude. Your nose is like the tower of Lebanon, which looks out toward Damascus.
022	SOL	007	005	Your head is like Carmel, and the hairs of your head are like the purple of the king, bound into pleats.
022	SOL	007	006	Most beloved one, how beautiful you are, and how graceful in delights!
022	SOL	007	007	Your stature is comparable to the palm tree, and your breasts to clusters of grapes.
022	SOL	007	008	Groom: I said, I will ascend to the palm tree, and take hold of its fruit. And your breasts will be like clusters of grapes on the vine. And the fragrance of your mouth will be like apples.
022	SOL	007	009	Bride: Your throat is like the finest wine: wine worthy for my beloved to drink, and for his lips and teeth to contemplate.
022	SOL	007	010	I am for my beloved, and his turning is to me.
022	SOL	007	011	Approach, my beloved. Let us go out into the field; let us linger in the villages.
022	SOL	007	012	Let us go up in the morning to the vineyards; let us see if the vineyard has flourished, if the flowers are ready to bear fruit, if the pomegranates have flourished. There I will give my breasts to you.
022	SOL	007	013	The mandrakes yield their fragrance. At our gates is every fruit. The new and the old, my beloved, I have kept for you.
022	SOL	008	001	Bride to Groom: Who will give you to me as my brother, feeding from the breasts of my mother, so that I may discover you outside, and may kiss you, and so that now no one may despise me?
022	SOL	008	002	I will take hold of you and lead you into my mother’s house. There you will teach me, and I will give you a cup of spiced wine, and of new wine from my pomegranates.
022	SOL	008	003	His left hand is under my head, and his right hand shall embrace me.
022	SOL	008	004	Groom to Chorus: I bind you by oath, O daughters of Jerusalem, not to disturb or awaken the beloved, until she wills.
022	SOL	008	005	Chorus to Groom: Who is she, who ascends from the desert, flowing with delights, leaning upon her beloved? Groom to Bride: Under the apple tree, I awakened you. There your mother was corrupted. There she who bore you was violated.
022	SOL	008	006	Set me like a seal upon your heart, like a seal upon your arm. For love is strong, like death, and envy is enduring, like hell: their lamps are made of fire and flames.
022	SOL	008	007	A multitude of waters cannot extinguish love, nor can a river overwhelm it. If a man were to give all the substance of his house in exchange for love, he would despise it as nothing.
022	SOL	008	008	Chorus: Our sister is little and has no breasts. What shall we do for our sister on the day when she is called upon?
022	SOL	008	009	If she is a wall, let us build a rampart of silver upon it. If she is a door, let us join it together with boards of cedar.
022	SOL	008	010	Bride to Chorus: I am a wall, and my breasts are like towers, since, in his presence, I have become like one who has discovered peace.
022	SOL	008	011	The peaceful one had a vineyard, in that which held the peoples. He handed it on to the caretakers; a man brought, in exchange for its fruit, a thousand pieces of silver.
022	SOL	008	012	Groom: My vineyard is before me. The thousand is for your peacefulness, and two hundred is for those who care for its fruit.
022	SOL	008	013	Bride to Groom: Your friends are attentive to those who have been dwelling in the gardens. Cause me to heed your voice.
022	SOL	008	014	Flee away, my beloved, and become like the doe and the young stag upon the mountains of aromatic plants.
